<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diwan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 636 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The diwan was a register and administrative office in Islamic states. Established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> Umar around 636 CE, it recorded the names of Muslim soldiers and their stipends, financed by conquest revenues, and became the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>'s fiscal administration. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, the diwan was the official responsible for finance.</p></div>
